From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: keep enhanced strobe across every controller rebuild

The board announces HS400 Enhanced Strobe and then does not run it. Either the
controller is configured for the mode or the mode is not offered; today it is
neither, and that is the non-determinism this track is clearing out.

THE MEASUREMENT
---------------
the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-20, test kernel 6.18.36-t0147 (main + `mmc-sunxi-stop-the-card-before-tearing-the-data-engine-down` + `mmc-sunxi-program-the-auto-cmd12-argument`
+ `mmc-sunxi-stop-writing-0xceaa-into-smhc-funs` + `arm64-dts-a733-cubie-a7s-emmc-enhanced-strobe`). The switch itself works:

  [ 2.775493] sunxi-mmc 4022000.mmc: HS400 enhanced strobe off, SMHC_CQE_CTRL 0x00000003
  [ 2.925003] sunxi-mmc 4022000.mmc: HS400 enhanced strobe on, SMHC_CQE_CTRL 0x00000103
  [ 2.957475] mmc2: new HS400 Enhanced strobe MMC card at address 0001

and /sys/kernel/debug/mmc2/ios keeps saying so: "timing spec: 10 (mmc HS400
enhanced strobe)", "bus width: 3 (8 bits)", "actual clock: 200000000 Hz".

In steady operation SMHC_CQE_CTRL (0x0402_21A0) reads 0x00000003 -- bit 8
DS_SAMP_RESP_EN is gone. Read three times with the device pinned awake
(power/control = on): straight after boot, immediately after a 64 MiB O_DIRECT
read, and after an idle suspend/resume. 0x00000003 every time, which is the
register's documented reset value (A733 UM 11.3.8.41, "Default Value:
0x0000_0003"; the bit is R/W with reset 0). The card frames its command
responses on the data strobe while the controller samples them on its own
clock.

WHO CLEARS IT
-------------
Not a stray write: nothing in the tree writes 0x1A0 except
sunxi_mmc_hs400_enhanced_strobe(), and the core calls that op exactly once per
mode switch (drivers/mmc/core/mmc.c mmc_select_hs400es(), and once from
core.c mmc_set_initial_state()). What clears it is a reset of the block, and
the driver does one on every idle. Line numbers are the series-applied tree,
drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c:

  :3005 sunxi_mmc_runtime_suspend()
        :3019 sunxi_mmc_reset_host()   -> :517 GCTRL soft + FIFO + DMA reset
        :3020 sunxi_mmc_disable()      -> :2634 reset_control_assert(host->reset)

pm_runtime autosuspend is 50 ms (:2941), so this runs whenever the host goes
idle. The module reset at :2634 returns the whole SMHC register file to its
reset values; the GCTRL reset at :517 is the second candidate and this patch
does not need to separate them, because both are answered at the same place.

And the rebuild path does not put the bit back:

  :2976 sunxi_mmc_runtime_resume()
        :2986 sunxi_mmc_init_host()          -> :530 FTRGL, TMOUT, IMASK,
                                                RINTR, DBGC, FUNS, DLBA,
                                                GCTRL -- nothing at 0x1A0
        :2987 sunxi_mmc_set_bus_width()
        :2988 sunxi_mmc_set_clk(host, &mmc->ios)

So the bit survives from 2.925 s until the first 50 ms of idle, and never
comes back.

THE OTHER HS400 BITS DO NOT HAVE THIS -- CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED
-------------------------------------------------------------
This was the second question the change had to ask before touching anything,
and the answer is no, so nothing about them changes here.

In code: :2988 sunxi_mmc_set_clk() -> sunxi_mmc_clk_set_rate() -> :1420
sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable(host, ios), which reprograms SMHC_CSDC, SMHC_NTSR,
SMHC_DS_DL and EMMC_DSBD from ios on every single pass, including every
runtime resume. They are written from ios each time, not latched once.

On silicon, from the other side -- the 2026-09-19 register sweep on the 8 GiB board, read
in ordinary operation long after boot:

  EMMC_DSBD  0x0402_210C = 0x80000000   HS400_MD_EN set
  SMHC_DS_DL 0x0402_2148 = 0x0b8b       the applied strobe delay
  SMHC_CQE_CTRL 0x0402_21A0 = 0x00000003

One sweep, same board, same steady state: the register file is being rebuilt,
and SMHC_CQE_CTRL is the one register the rebuild misses. (That sweep predates
`arm64-dts-a733-cubie-a7s-emmc-enhanced-strobe`, so its 0x3 is "never written"; the 2026-09-20 reading above is the one
that shows the loss. Both are quoted, neither is mixed up with the other.)

WHAT THIS CHANGES
-----------------
The one-bit write moves into sunxi_mmc_hs400_es_write() and gets a second
caller: sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable(), beside the other four HS400 registers. That
is the point where controller state is rebuilt, so the ES bit is now rebuilt
with everything else. Leaving HS400 clears it in the same teardown branch that
undoes HS400_MD_EN, CSDC, NTSR and DS_DL.

It is derived from ios->enhanced_strobe, not cached in struct sunxi_mmc_host.
That is deliberate and it is the deviation from the obvious design:

- struct mmc_ios is already this driver's authority for every other mode bit.
  A copy in host state would be a second thing that can go stale, for no gain.
- The core keeps the flag set for exactly as long as the mode lasts: set in
  mmc_select_hs400es() (core/mmc.c:1457), cleared in mmc_set_initial_state()
  (core/core.c:1000) when the card is reinitialised.
- Both callers of sunxi_mmc_set_clk() pass the core's own ios -- set_ios
  (:1825) and runtime_resume (:2988, &mmc->ios) -- so there is no path where
  the flag read here is not the current one.

Ordering, because it is not obvious: mmc_select_hs400es() calls
mmc_set_timing(MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS400) *before* it sets ios.enhanced_strobe. So
on the first pass sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable() writes the bit as 0 and the
->hs400_enhanced_strobe call immediately behind it writes 1 -- which is
exactly the pair of log lines at 2.775 and 2.925 above. Every pass after that
has the flag set and writes 1 directly.

WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM
----------------------
- No throughput claim. The strobe frames the response, not the payload. If the
  rate moves at all, that is a finding, not the goal -- see the falsifier.
- It does not touch HS400_MD_EN, CSDC, NTSR or DS_DL, and it does not change
  when the data-strobe sweep runs.
- It does not decide whether SMHC_CQE_CTRL needs the CQE block powered. The bit
  took the write at 2.925 s with no CQE running, which settles the question for
  the write; whether it stays taken across a rebuild is what this patch makes
  measurable.

CROSS-READ -- WHAT THE OTHER DRIVERS DO WITH THEIR ES STATE
-----------------------------------------------------------
- dw_mmc: nothing to take. No dw_mmc variant in 6.18.36 implements
  ->hs400_enhanced_strobe (grep over drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc*.c: zero hits), so
  it carries no ES state across resume.
- mtk-sd: carries it, and carries it incompletely -- the same defect in a
  milder form, which is why it is named rather than followed.
  msdc_hs400_enhanced_strobe() (mtk-sd.c:2713) writes EMMC50_CFG0, EMMC50_CFG1,
  CQHCI_SETTING and EMMC51_CFG0. Its runtime PM saves and restores raw register
  values around the gated HCLK -- msdc_save_reg()/msdc_restore_reg()
  (mtk-sd.c:3210/:3239) -- and struct msdc_save_para (mtk-sd.c:415) holds
  emmc50_cfg0 and emmc50_cfg3 but not emmc50_cfg1, not CQHCI_SETTING and not
  EMMC51_CFG0. So three of the four registers its ES path writes are outside
  what it restores.
  Two things follow, both deliberate here: save-and-restore of raw values is
  not copied (it can carry a stale value forward, where reprogramming from ios
  cannot), and the ES write is placed on the rebuild path rather than in a
  save/restore pair.
- A733 vendor BSP (sunxi-mmc-v4p5x.c, the v4p6x path this board binds): it
  offers enhanced strobe on no path at all. No ->hs400_enhanced_strobe anywhere
  in the BSP; SDXC_REG_CQE_CTRL is defined (sunxi-mmc.h:116) and never written;
  the only three mentions of MMC_CAP2_HS400_ES are clears inside
  sunxi_mmc_select_timing() (sunxi-mmc.c:172/:180/:187), a timing-override
  helper, and nothing ever sets it. Its HS400 register pair is reprogrammed
  from ios on the clock path (sunxi-mmc-v4p5x.c:816-833, inside
  sunxi_mmc_clk_set_rate_for_sdmmc_v4p5x(): EDSD HS400_MD_EN + CSDC), which is the same idiom this patch extends the ES bit into -- so the
  vendor gets the structure right for the two bits it has and simply does not
  have this one. Nothing to port; nothing it does better.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
-----------------------
- Applies clean at fuzz 0 on a fresh unpack with the full series in series
  order: yes, checked exactly the way scripts/build.sh applies it.
- Builds: not asserted here.
- Silicon: NOT proven. The observable on the 8 GiB board, which is the measurement that
  found the defect, repeated:
    * busybox devmem 0x040221A0 32 reads bit 8 set (0x00000103) with the device
      pinned awake (echo on > /sys/devices/platform/soc/4022000.mmc/power/control)
      at all three points: right after boot, right after a 64 MiB O_DIRECT
      read, and after an idle suspend/resume cycle
    * /sys/kernel/debug/mmc2/ios still reads "timing spec: 10 (mmc HS400
      enhanced strobe)"
    * sequential write does not regress -- 156 MiB/s or above, the rate the
      same board and kernel generation gives today -- and verify is clean
  What falsifies it: bit 8 still reads 0, and then the loss sits somewhere this
  patch does not reach; or the write rate falls, and then the bit costs
  something and the question is a different one.

ISSUE
-----
No separate issue: the finding and its fix ship together in this patch.
It closes the operational half of the hardware proof still open for `mmc-sunxi-stop-claiming-the-controller-cannot-do-enhanced-strobe` and `arm64-dts-a733-cubie-a7s-emmc-enhanced-strobe` -- ES announced is now ES configured, for as long as the
mode lasts.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -1614,11 +1614,51 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * Enhanced strobe, one bit: SMHC_CQE_CTRL[8] DS_SAMP_RESP_EN (UM 11.3.8.41,
+ * offset 0x01A0, register reset value 0x0000_0003, the bit itself R/W reset 0).
+ *
+ * Split out because it has two callers that must agree. ->hs400_enhanced_strobe
+ * is where the core announces the mode, and sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable() is where
+ * the controller state is rebuilt afterwards -- every runtime resume, for as
+ * long as the card stays in HS400.
+ */
+static void sunxi_mmc_hs400_es_write(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host, bool on)
+{
+	u32 rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_CQE_CTRL);
+
+	if (on)
+		rval |= SDXC_DS_SAMP_RESP_EN;
+	else
+		rval &= ~SDXC_DS_SAMP_RESP_EN;
+
+	mmc_writel(host, REG_CQE_CTRL, rval);
+
+	/*
+	 * The re-apply is silent by design -- it runs on every runtime resume,
+	 * which on this host is every 50 ms of idle. Armable without a rebuild
+	 * when the question comes back:
+	 *   dyndbg="func sunxi_mmc_hs400_es_write +p"
+	 * The standing observable stays the register itself.
+	 */
+	dev_dbg(mmc_dev(host->mmc), "HS400 ES %s, SMHC_CQE_CTRL 0x%08x\n",
+		on ? "on" : "off", mmc_readl(host, REG_CQE_CTRL));
+}
+
+/*
  * Program the HS400-specific timing bits. Called from the clock-setup path
  * with the card clock gated, only on controllers that carry the data-strobe
  * delay (cfg->can_hs400). Everything is gated on the HS400 timing, so the SD
  * controller (which never selects HS400) is untouched. Register recipe from
  * the A733 UM ch. 11.3.
+ *
+ * This is also the re-apply point. Runtime suspend asserts the controller's
+ * reset (sunxi_mmc_runtime_suspend -> sunxi_mmc_disable ->
+ * reset_control_assert), so after every resume the whole SMHC register file is
+ * back at its reset values; sunxi_mmc_runtime_resume() rebuilds it through
+ * sunxi_mmc_init_host() and sunxi_mmc_set_clk(), and set_clk lands here. Every
+ * HS400 bit therefore has to be written from ios on each pass, not once at
+ * mode-switch time -- which is exactly why ios->enhanced_strobe is read here
+ * and not cached.
  */
 static void sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host,
 				   struct mmc_ios *ios)
@@ -1650,6 +1690,14 @@
 		rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_DS_DL_REG);
 		rval &= ~(SDXC_DS_DL_SW_MASK | SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_EN);
 		mmc_writel(host, REG_DS_DL_REG, rval);
+
+		/*
+		 * Enhanced strobe is part of that teardown: it only ever
+		 * applies inside HS400, and leaving it set while the response
+		 * path goes back to the controller's own sampling clock would
+		 * be the same defect in the other direction.
+		 */
+		sunxi_mmc_hs400_es_write(host, false);
 		return;
 	}
 
@@ -1718,6 +1766,19 @@
 	else
 		rval &= ~SDXC_HALF_START_BIT;
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_EMMC_DSBD, rval);
+
+	/*
+	 * Enhanced strobe, from ios, on the same pass as the rest. The core
+	 * sets ios->enhanced_strobe in mmc_select_hs400es() *after* the
+	 * mmc_set_timing(MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS400) that brings us here the first
+	 * time, so the first pass writes 0 and the ->hs400_enhanced_strobe
+	 * call right behind it writes 1. From then on the flag is set and
+	 * every later pass -- each runtime resume -- reapplies it, which is
+	 * the whole point: without it the bit is lost at the first idle
+	 * suspend and the card runs HS400ES against a controller that is no
+	 * longer sampling responses on the strobe.
+	 */
+	sunxi_mmc_hs400_es_write(host, ios->enhanced_strobe);
 }
 
 static int sunxi_mmc_clk_set_rate(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host,
@@ -2340,20 +2401,16 @@
  *
  * The rest of the HS400 register recipe is unchanged and still comes from
  * sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable(), which the core reaches through mmc_set_timing()
- * on the ES path exactly as on the plain HS400 path.
+ * on the ES path exactly as on the plain HS400 path -- and which reapplies
+ * this bit too, because the core calls this op exactly once per mode switch
+ * while the controller loses the register on every runtime suspend.
  */
 static void sunxi_mmc_hs400_enhanced_strobe(struct mmc_host *mmc,
 					    struct mmc_ios *ios)
 {
 	struct sunxi_mmc_host *host = mmc_priv(mmc);
-	u32 rval;
 
-	rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_CQE_CTRL);
-	if (ios->enhanced_strobe)
-		rval |= SDXC_DS_SAMP_RESP_EN;
-	else
-		rval &= ~SDXC_DS_SAMP_RESP_EN;
-	mmc_writel(host, REG_CQE_CTRL, rval);
+	sunxi_mmc_hs400_es_write(host, ios->enhanced_strobe);
 
 	dev_info(mmc_dev(mmc), "HS400 enhanced strobe %s, SMHC_CQE_CTRL 0x%08x\n",
 		 ios->enhanced_strobe ? "on" : "off",
